WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.099
Of all the dislocations caused

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.199
by government responses to COVID,

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.539
arguably, none were more disruptive

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.399
to everyday life than the shutting

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.469
down of in-person education

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.569
for the country's 50 million

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.069
K-through-12 students and their

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:14.939
parents.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.409
Teachers unions fought to keep

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.389
schools online, even as evidence

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.069
piled up that remote learning was

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.019
disastrous, especially

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.009
for poor kids.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.899
And as the experiences of other

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.549
developed countries, which mostly

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.049
continue to operate in-person,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.999
demonstrated that schools weren't

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.859
a major source of infection.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.229
The results were easy to predict.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.209
Historic declines in reading

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:37.199
and math scores.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.939
Major school districts continue to

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.889
alienate parents with Washington,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.839
D.C., recently decreeing

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.939
that kids ages 12 and older

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.039
would need to be vaccinated even

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.109
for remote learning, a measure

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.999
that would have barred 40%

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.709
of the city's black teens from

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.089
getting an education.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:55.769
The policy was thankfully pushed

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:58.109
back until January of 2023.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.359
But it's still on the books, lurking

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.219
like a bully at the far end of the

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.499
home life. More parents than ever

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.329
have exited major urban school

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.499
districts, and school choice

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.059
proponents are building on recent

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.279
policy victories, such as Arizona's

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.199
new law, in which money follows

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.259
the child with up to $7,000

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.149
that can be used that any public or

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.439
private school in the state.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:20.999
The politics of school choice are

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.009
already a major issue in the midterm

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.529
elections and will be again in 2024,

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.329
especially as internal polls

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.009
conducted by the American Federation

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.019
of Teachers find for the first time

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.969
that voters in battleground states

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.499
are more likely to agree that

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.959
Republicans are better on education

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.099
than Democrats.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.079
So what happens next at

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.759
Freedom Fest? The annual July

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:42.929
gathering in Las Vegas.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.729
Reason talked with Corey DeAngelis,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.199
a senior fellow at the American

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.089
Federation of Children, and a senior

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.069
fellow at Reason Foundation, which

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.629
publishes Reason.com.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.399
We talked about how COVID has

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.289
permanently reshaped the education

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.329
landscape. Why top-down bans

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:58.769
on critical race theory are

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.869
ineffective and anti-freedom?

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.119
Why some red states like Texas

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.859
are terrible on choice?

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.689
And why all of us, whether we have

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:08.698
kids in K-through-12 schools or not,

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.709
should be invested in radical

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:11.710
reform.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.529
Corey DeAngelis, thanks for talking

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:19.149
to Reason.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.439
Thank you so much for having me.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.269
So there is a

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.939
leaked poll that is

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.979
really kind of, you know, that

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.959
sheds a weird light and a kind

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.759
of wonderful light for school choice

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.249
people on,

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.499
you know, school choice issues

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.479
coming into the midterm elections

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.099
and beyond. It was it was

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.659
commissioned by the American

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.669
Federation for Teachers.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.529
So this was a poll that was supposed

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.089
to show one of the biggest teachers

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:48.159
unions how well they are positioned

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:49.269
going into things.

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:50.559
What's the poll say?

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.539
And, you know, what's the fallout?

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:53.649
Yeah, it doesn't look good for the

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:55.539
teachers unions is essentially an

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.759
epic self-own by Randi Weingarten

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.379
and her union, the American

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:00.819
Federation of Teachers.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.889
So this is a poll of likely voters

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.329
in battleground states like

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.089
Pennsylvania and others conducted

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:09.039
by or for is for the

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:10.569
American Federation of Teachers.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:12.370
And the top line finding was that

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.249
likely voters in these battleground

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:17.109
states were more likely to support

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:19.089
Republicans on education

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:20.469
as opposed to Democrats.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:22.149
And so that's that's big finding

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:23.559
itself.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.119
Usually, Republicans are leaning on

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.039
things like the economy and jobs,

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.419
not so much on education.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.339
We did see this happen with Glenn

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:31.479
Youngkin when he won the

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.459
gubernatorial race in Virginia

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:34.929
against Terry McAuliffe, who was the

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.299
Senate, was a former incumbent

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.129
in the state, in a state that went

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.079
ten percentage points to Biden,

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:42.879
Glenn Youngkin won by two percentage

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.499
points. And according to Washington

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.539
Post exit polling, he won with

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.609
education voters by about

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:50.229
six percentage points.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:51.519
And education was the number two

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:52.609
issue in that election.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:54.639
Now, that was schools coming really

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.649
coming directly out of COVID

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.869
and lockdowns and just arbitrariness

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:01.299
and and a broad sense of

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:03.279
teachers not caring about

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.839
education during COVID.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.879
They were more, you know, so

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.829
now, you know, we're we're, you

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:10.689
know, ten months past, all

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:12.549
of that kind of stuff.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.379
What what are the complaints

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.509
that parents have about

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:18.249
educators right now?

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.078
So people's parents had

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:21.518
their eyes opened.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:22.439
Why? Why?

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.129
Because of the pandemic school

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.989
closures, the union induced school

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:27.369
closures. They got to see what was

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:28.839
going on in the classroom.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.789
And the problems, as I

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:32.889
see it, are just symptoms

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:34.779
of the larger issues that we forced

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:36.069
millions of families to send their

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:37.779
kids to a one size fits

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:39.039
all government-run school system.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.869
That, by definition, isn't going

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.759
to work for families who

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:43.899
disagree about how they want their

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.039
kids raised, how they want their

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.089
kids to be educated.

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.859
So whether it's mask mandates or

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:49.119
not, whether it's remote versus

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.589
in-person instruction, whether it's

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.599
this curriculum or that curriculum

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.489
or anything else, by

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.049
definition, if families are paying

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:57.519
attention to what's going on,

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.529
there's going to be a large segment

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:01.909
of the population or at least a

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:03.759
a sizable

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:04.749
part of the population that's not

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:05.769
going to be happy for whatever

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.899
reason, just by definition.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:09.399
Yeah. And I want to also point out

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.289
that this poll also found

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.269
that Randi Weingarten

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:14.379
has been saying that it's been the

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.870
Republicans stoking this,

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:18.939
bringing politics into the

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:19.899
classroom.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.249
But they also asked in their own

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:22.989
poll, you know what?

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.279
What's the biggest issue with public

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:25.869
schools? The number one issue was

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:27.669
overly politicized classrooms.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.649
But then they also asked is who

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:30.939
is more responsible? The Republicans

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:32.499
are the Democrats. And by about 5 to

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.329
6 percentage points, these likely

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:35.649
voters were saying it's more likely

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:37.779
to be the Democrats

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:39.879
that are creating the issues with

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.609
politics in the classroom as opposed

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:42.610
to Republicans.

